    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm having some trouble with a set of walnut cabinet carcasses I'm working on. I'm cutting through dovetails, but I'm getting consistent splintering at the tips of the pins every time I finish the cut. The wood is quite stable, so I don't think it's a moisture issue. Any ideas on what might be causing this or how to fix my setup?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Splintering at dovetail tips, even in stable walnut, can often be caused by dull bits or aggressive feed rates during cutting. Ensure your dovetail saw blade is razor-sharp and is properly tensioned for a clean shear cut. Try using a very light, consistent pressure and slower feeding speed as you finish the cut to allow the blade to exit the wood smoothly instead of tearing it.",
      "source": "target",
