    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for the tip on the feed rate. I've checked the saw and it's definitely sharp, and I'm already going pretty slow. I've also tried using a backing board to prevent breakout on the exit side, but the splintering is still happening right at the very point where the pin meets the socket. Could it be an issue with how I'm angling the chisel when I'm cleaning out the waste, or is there something else about the grain structure in walnut that I should be looking out for?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Since you've addressed the saw and feed rate, the issue might be the final waste removal. When cleaning the waste, ensure your chisels are perfectly sharp and angled precisely to meet the cut faces, removing only slivers of waste, not digging into the wood. Walnut can sometimes have subtle grain variations; be extra mindful of changes in direction as you clean near the tips to avoid forcing the chisel against a grain change.",
      "source": "target",
