    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I'll double check my chisel angle and try to be more delicate with the grain direction. It's just frustrating because everything seems so perfect until that very last second where it just breaks away. Do you think there's any specific way to prep the wood itself before the cut to make those tips more resilient, like a specific way to sand or maybe something about the way the grain is oriented in the board?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While sanding doesn't significantly prevent cut tear-out on the tips, ensuring the raw stock is properly acclimatized can help reduce internal stress. Pay very close attention to the grain orientation; always aim for the dovetails to run perpendicular to the strongest grain lines available. If the grain is highly figure-intensive in the joint area, it will naturally be more prone to tear-out regardless of your technique.",
      "source": "target",
